1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,849
If you grew up in the fifties

2
00:00:01,849 --> 00:00:04,337
or sixties, close your eyes and picture

3
00:00:04,337 --> 00:00:05,932
your grandmother's house.

4
00:00:06,112 --> 00:00:08,098
The smell of something baking.

5
00:00:08,278 --> 00:00:10,031
A candy dish on the table.

6
00:00:10,211 --> 00:00:12,045
A clock ticking in the hallway.

7
00:00:12,225 --> 00:00:14,651
Crocheted doilies on every chair.

8
00:00:14,831 --> 00:00:16,139
And your grandmother,

9
00:00:16,139 --> 00:00:18,381
in a cotton housedress and an apron,

10
00:00:18,381 --> 00:00:20,311
waiting at the door to hug you.

11
00:00:20,491 --> 00:00:22,322
For millions of baby boomers,

12
00:00:22,322 --> 00:00:24,469
that house was the safest place in

13
00:00:24,469 --> 00:00:25,101
the world.

14
00:00:25,451 --> 00:00:27,211
But what was life like for the

15
00:00:27,211 --> 00:00:28,620
grandparents themselves?

16
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:31,185
If you were a grandparent in nineteen

17
00:00:31,185 --> 00:00:33,506
sixty, you were probably born in the

18
00:00:33,506 --> 00:00:36,020
eighteen nineties or the first years of

19
00:00:36,020 --> 00:00:37,438
the nineteen hundreds.

20
00:00:37,618 --> 00:00:40,024
You had grown up with horses and

21
00:00:40,024 --> 00:00:42,506
buggies, oil lamps and outhouses.

22
00:00:42,686 --> 00:00:45,726
And now you were watching your grandchildren

23
00:00:45,726 --> 00:00:47,384
grow up with television,

24
00:00:47,384 --> 00:00:49,803
jet airplanes and rockets to space.

25
00:00:50,153 --> 00:00:52,493
Today, let's look at the world through

26
00:00:52,493 --> 00:00:53,171
their eyes.

27
00:00:53,351 --> 00:00:55,511
The grandparents of the baby boom.

28
00:00:55,691 --> 00:00:57,329
What they had lived through,

29
00:00:57,329 --> 00:00:59,260
how they lived in nineteen sixty,

30
00:00:59,260 --> 00:01:01,542
and what they gave their grandchildren.

31
00:01:01,722 --> 00:01:03,960
If you had grandparents like these,

32
00:01:03,960 --> 00:01:06,133
I think you'll see them clearly by

33
00:01:06,133 --> 00:01:06,645
the end.

34
00:01:06,995 --> 00:01:09,604
Think about what a grandparent of nineteen

35
00:01:09,604 --> 00:01:11,592
sixty had already lived through.

36
00:01:11,772 --> 00:01:15,159
As children, many had no electricity,

37
00:01:15,159 --> 00:01:17,356
no telephone and no car.

38
00:01:17,536 --> 00:01:20,321
They were teenagers or young adults during

39
00:01:20,321 --> 00:01:21,646
the First World War.

40
00:01:21,826 --> 00:01:24,374
Many young men served in France in

41
00:01:24,374 --> 00:01:26,772
nineteen seventeen and eighteen.

42
00:01:26,952 --> 00:01:29,757
And then came the influenza pandemic of

43
00:01:29,757 --> 00:01:33,280
nineteen eighteen, which killed about six hundred

44
00:01:33,280 --> 00:01:35,869
and seventy-five thousand Americans.

45
00:01:36,219 --> 00:01:37,809
Then came the Great Depression.

46
00:01:37,989 --> 00:01:40,141
Many of them were in their thirties

47
00:01:40,141 --> 00:01:42,662
when the stock market crashed in nineteen

48
00:01:42,662 --> 00:01:43,399
twenty-nine.

49
00:01:43,579 --> 00:01:44,890
They lost jobs,

50
00:01:44,890 --> 00:01:47,074
farms, savings and homes.

51
00:01:47,254 --> 00:01:49,706
They raised their children on beans,

52
00:01:49,706 --> 00:01:51,341
bread and hand-me-downs.

53
00:01:51,521 --> 00:01:54,243
Many never fully trusted banks again,

54
00:01:54,243 --> 00:01:56,893
and kept cash hidden in coffee cans,

55
00:01:56,893 --> 00:01:59,763
mattresses and cookie jars for the rest

56
00:01:59,763 --> 00:02:00,867
of their lives.

57
00:02:01,217 --> 00:02:03,104
And then the Second World War,

58
00:02:03,104 --> 00:02:05,181
when they sent their own sons off

59
00:02:05,181 --> 00:02:07,069
to fight, and hung a blue star

60
00:02:07,069 --> 00:02:07,950
in the window.

61
00:02:08,130 --> 00:02:10,197
Some of those stars turned gold.

62
00:02:10,377 --> 00:02:11,747
By nineteen sixty,

63
00:02:11,747 --> 00:02:14,259
they had survived two world wars,

64
00:02:14,259 --> 00:02:16,391
a pandemic and a depression.

65
00:02:16,571 --> 00:02:18,550
No wonder they seemed so calm about

66
00:02:18,550 --> 00:02:20,925
the small problems of their grandchildren.

67
00:02:21,275 --> 00:02:23,565
They had also seen the world change

68
00:02:23,565 --> 00:02:25,791
faster than any generation before.

69
00:02:25,971 --> 00:02:28,217
They had seen the first automobiles,

70
00:02:28,217 --> 00:02:29,465
the first airplanes,

71
00:02:29,465 --> 00:02:31,774
the first radios and the first movies

72
00:02:31,774 --> 00:02:32,461
with sound.

73
00:02:32,641 --> 00:02:35,245
They had watched electric lights come to

74
00:02:35,245 --> 00:02:37,850
their towns, and indoor plumbing come to

75
00:02:37,850 --> 00:02:38,632
their homes.

76
00:02:38,812 --> 00:02:41,038
Some of them had seen their first

77
00:02:41,038 --> 00:02:43,737
television set only a few years earlier,

78
00:02:43,737 --> 00:02:44,884
in their sixties.

79
00:02:45,234 --> 00:02:46,622
In nineteen sixty,

80
00:02:46,622 --> 00:02:49,707
many grandparents still lived in the old

81
00:02:49,707 --> 00:02:50,632
family home.

82
00:02:50,812 --> 00:02:52,054
A big farmhouse,

83
00:02:52,054 --> 00:02:54,539
or a two-story house in an older

84
00:02:54,539 --> 00:02:55,548
part of town,

85
00:02:55,548 --> 00:02:57,023
with a front porch,

86
00:02:57,023 --> 00:02:59,352
a big kitchen and a garden out

87
00:02:59,352 --> 00:02:59,740
back.

88
00:02:59,920 --> 00:03:01,954
They had lived there for thirty or

89
00:03:01,954 --> 00:03:02,672
forty years.

90
00:03:02,852 --> 00:03:05,335
Every room was full of memories,

91
00:03:05,335 --> 00:03:07,275
and nothing ever changed.

92
00:03:07,626 --> 00:03:10,215
Many others lived with their grown children.

93
00:03:10,395 --> 00:03:12,844
It was common for a widowed grandmother

94
00:03:12,844 --> 00:03:14,477
to move in with her son or

95
00:03:14,477 --> 00:03:15,607
daughter's family.

96
00:03:15,787 --> 00:03:18,419
She might have a small bedroom upstairs,

97
00:03:18,419 --> 00:03:20,130
or a room off the kitchen.

98
00:03:20,310 --> 00:03:21,479
She helped cook,

99
00:03:21,479 --> 00:03:24,257
watched the children and told stories.

100
00:03:24,437 --> 00:03:27,018
Three generations under one roof was a

101
00:03:27,018 --> 00:03:28,988
normal part of American life.

102
00:03:29,338 --> 00:03:33,152
Some grandparents moved to Florida or Arizona,

103
00:03:33,152 --> 00:03:34,977
but in nineteen sixty,

104
00:03:34,977 --> 00:03:36,884
that was still unusual.

105
00:03:37,064 --> 00:03:38,640
Most stayed close to home,

106
00:03:38,640 --> 00:03:41,066
close to their church and their friends,

107
00:03:41,066 --> 00:03:43,066
and close to their grandchildren.

108
00:03:43,246 --> 00:03:45,902
On Sundays, the whole family came over

109
00:03:45,902 --> 00:03:46,671
for dinner.

110
00:03:46,851 --> 00:03:49,387
The grandparents' house was the center of

111
00:03:49,387 --> 00:03:50,067
the family.

112
00:03:50,418 --> 00:03:53,288
Money was tight for many grandparents in

113
00:03:53,288 --> 00:03:54,365
nineteen sixty.

114
00:03:54,545 --> 00:03:57,737
Social Security had only started paying monthly

115
00:03:57,737 --> 00:03:59,571
benefits in nineteen forty,

116
00:03:59,571 --> 00:04:01,065
and in nineteen sixty,

117
00:04:01,065 --> 00:04:03,646
the average check for a retired worker

118
00:04:03,646 --> 00:04:06,294
was about seventy-four dollars a month.

119
00:04:06,474 --> 00:04:08,756
Many older people had no pension at

120
00:04:08,756 --> 00:04:09,017
all.

121
00:04:09,197 --> 00:04:10,822
And there was no Medicare yet.

122
00:04:11,002 --> 00:04:14,044
That wouldn't come until nineteen sixty-five.

123
00:04:14,394 --> 00:04:16,307
In nineteen fifty-nine,

124
00:04:16,307 --> 00:04:19,968
about one in three Americans over sixty-five

125
00:04:19,968 --> 00:04:21,383
lived in poverty.

126
00:04:21,563 --> 00:04:23,792
Many depended on their children for help.

127
00:04:23,972 --> 00:04:26,942
Others kept working as long as their

128
00:04:26,942 --> 00:04:29,170
bodies allowed, as farmers,

129
00:04:29,170 --> 00:04:32,470
shopkeepers, carpenters or seamstresses.

130
00:04:32,650 --> 00:04:35,628
The idea of retiring at sixty-five and

131
00:04:35,628 --> 00:04:38,293
traveling the world was still new.

132
00:04:38,643 --> 00:04:40,872
But they were masters at making do.

133
00:04:41,052 --> 00:04:43,357
Grandmothers saved string,

134
00:04:43,357 --> 00:04:45,573
rubber bands, bread bags,

135
00:04:45,573 --> 00:04:47,878
aluminum foil and buttons.

136
00:04:48,058 --> 00:04:49,365
They mended socks,

137
00:04:49,365 --> 00:04:52,633
patched clothes, and made quilts from scraps.

138
00:04:52,813 --> 00:04:54,810
They grew vegetables and canned them.

139
00:04:54,990 --> 00:04:57,228
Nothing was thrown away that might be

140
00:04:57,228 --> 00:04:58,136
useful someday.

141
00:04:58,316 --> 00:04:59,289
Use it up,

142
00:04:59,289 --> 00:05:00,457
wear it out,

143
00:05:00,457 --> 00:05:01,528
make it do,

144
00:05:01,528 --> 00:05:02,890
or do without.

145
00:05:03,241 --> 00:05:06,661
Many grandparents also had firm opinions about

146
00:05:06,661 --> 00:05:07,107
money.

147
00:05:07,287 --> 00:05:09,378
Never buy what you can't pay cash

148
00:05:09,378 --> 00:05:09,632
for.

149
00:05:09,812 --> 00:05:12,138
Always keep something put away for a

150
00:05:12,138 --> 00:05:12,784
rainy day.

151
00:05:12,964 --> 00:05:15,481
Don't trust anything that sounds too good

152
00:05:15,481 --> 00:05:16,157
to be true.

153
00:05:16,337 --> 00:05:18,981
They watched their grown children buy cars

154
00:05:18,981 --> 00:05:20,618
and televisions on credit,

155
00:05:20,618 --> 00:05:22,002
and shook their heads.

156
00:05:22,182 --> 00:05:24,919
Their grandchildren didn't always listen,

157
00:05:24,919 --> 00:05:26,989
but many later wished they had.

158
00:05:27,339 --> 00:05:28,990
What was a day like for a

159
00:05:28,990 --> 00:05:30,972
grandmother in nineteen sixty?

160
00:05:31,152 --> 00:05:32,346
She rose early,

161
00:05:32,346 --> 00:05:34,415
out of a lifetime's habit.

162
00:05:34,595 --> 00:05:36,688
She cooked a big breakfast,

163
00:05:36,688 --> 00:05:38,007
with eggs, bacon,

164
00:05:38,007 --> 00:05:40,798
biscuits and coffee boiled in an old

165
00:05:40,798 --> 00:05:41,108
pot.

166
00:05:41,288 --> 00:05:42,539
She swept the porch,

167
00:05:42,539 --> 00:05:44,602
fed the chickens if she had them,

168
00:05:44,602 --> 00:05:46,666
and worked in her garden while it

169
00:05:46,666 --> 00:05:47,604
was still cool.

170
00:05:47,954 --> 00:05:49,039
In the afternoon,

171
00:05:49,039 --> 00:05:49,997
she might bake.

172
00:05:50,177 --> 00:05:52,958
Bread, pies, cookies,

173
00:05:52,958 --> 00:05:53,753
cakes.

174
00:05:53,933 --> 00:05:56,684
Many grandmothers never measured anything.

175
00:05:56,864 --> 00:05:58,197
A handful of this,

176
00:05:58,197 --> 00:05:59,381
a pinch of that,

177
00:05:59,381 --> 00:06:01,009
until it looked right.

178
00:06:01,189 --> 00:06:04,024
Their grandchildren later tried to write down

179
00:06:04,024 --> 00:06:06,797
the recipes, and found it almost impossible.

180
00:06:06,977 --> 00:06:08,660
The secret ingredient,

181
00:06:08,660 --> 00:06:11,644
many said, was the grandmother herself.

182
00:06:11,994 --> 00:06:13,008
In the evening,

183
00:06:13,008 --> 00:06:15,441
she listened to the radio or watched

184
00:06:15,441 --> 00:06:16,185
television.

185
00:06:16,365 --> 00:06:19,193
Lawrence Welk on Saturday nights was a

186
00:06:19,193 --> 00:06:21,427
favorite of many grandparents.

187
00:06:21,607 --> 00:06:23,250
So were Ed Sullivan,

188
00:06:23,250 --> 00:06:25,879
Gunsmoke and religious programs.

189
00:06:26,059 --> 00:06:27,507
She read the Bible,

190
00:06:27,507 --> 00:06:29,183
did crossword puzzles,

191
00:06:29,183 --> 00:06:32,003
and wrote letters to her children and

192
00:06:32,003 --> 00:06:33,907
grandchildren in careful,

193
00:06:33,907 --> 00:06:35,127
flowing cursive.

194
00:06:35,477 --> 00:06:36,788
And she went to church.

195
00:06:36,968 --> 00:06:38,448
For many grandmothers,

196
00:06:38,448 --> 00:06:40,667
the church was the center of life

197
00:06:40,667 --> 00:06:41,810
outside the home.

198
00:06:41,990 --> 00:06:45,194
Sunday services, Wednesday prayer meetings,

199
00:06:45,194 --> 00:06:46,982
the ladies' aid society,

200
00:06:46,982 --> 00:06:50,186
the quilting circle and the church suppers.

201
00:06:50,366 --> 00:06:53,548
She brought casseroles to every funeral and

202
00:06:53,548 --> 00:06:55,324
pies to every bake sale.

203
00:06:55,504 --> 00:06:57,740
Her faith had carried her through the

204
00:06:57,740 --> 00:06:59,855
hardest times, and she never let go

205
00:06:59,855 --> 00:07:00,218
of it.

206
00:07:00,568 --> 00:07:04,221
Grandfathers of nineteen sixty were often quiet

207
00:07:04,221 --> 00:07:05,931
men with strong hands.

208
00:07:06,111 --> 00:07:07,925
Many had worked on farms,

209
00:07:07,925 --> 00:07:10,683
in mines, on railroads or in factories

210
00:07:10,683 --> 00:07:12,207
since they were boys.

211
00:07:12,387 --> 00:07:14,778
They had pocket watches and suspenders,

212
00:07:14,778 --> 00:07:17,414
and a pocketknife they used for everything.

213
00:07:17,594 --> 00:07:20,461
Many chewed tobacco or smoked a pipe.

214
00:07:20,812 --> 00:07:23,377
Grandpa might spend his mornings in the

215
00:07:23,377 --> 00:07:26,141
workshop, fixing things for the neighbors.

216
00:07:26,321 --> 00:07:29,232
His afternoons might be at the barbershop,

217
00:07:29,232 --> 00:07:32,005
the feed store or the courthouse square,

218
00:07:32,005 --> 00:07:34,570
where the old men gathered on benches

219
00:07:34,570 --> 00:07:36,372
to talk about the weather,

220
00:07:36,372 --> 00:07:38,244
the crops and the old days.

221
00:07:38,424 --> 00:07:39,399
In the evening,

222
00:07:39,399 --> 00:07:41,090
he sat on the porch in his

223
00:07:41,090 --> 00:07:42,000
rocking chair.

224
00:07:42,350 --> 00:07:44,683
He took the grandchildren fishing.

225
00:07:44,863 --> 00:07:46,347
He taught them to whittle,

226
00:07:46,347 --> 00:07:47,204
to bait a hook,

227
00:07:47,204 --> 00:07:48,973
to drive a nail and to whistle.

228
00:07:49,153 --> 00:07:51,112
He told stories about the war,

229
00:07:51,112 --> 00:07:52,288
or the Depression,

230
00:07:52,288 --> 00:07:54,574
or what the town looked like before

231
00:07:54,574 --> 00:07:55,945
the paved roads came.

232
00:07:56,125 --> 00:07:57,799
And he slipped them a quarter or

233
00:07:57,799 --> 00:07:59,526
a stick of gum when their parents

234
00:07:59,526 --> 00:08:00,363
weren't looking.

235
00:08:00,713 --> 00:08:04,443
Grandparents also had very definite ideas about

236
00:08:04,443 --> 00:08:05,078
manners.

237
00:08:05,258 --> 00:08:07,905
Children said yes ma'am and no sir.

238
00:08:08,085 --> 00:08:10,438
You didn't speak at the table unless

239
00:08:10,438 --> 00:08:11,092
spoken to.

240
00:08:11,272 --> 00:08:13,331
You wrote a thank-you note for every

241
00:08:13,331 --> 00:08:15,103
gift, in your best handwriting.

242
00:08:15,283 --> 00:08:17,257
You stood up when an adult entered

243
00:08:17,257 --> 00:08:17,779
the room.

244
00:08:17,959 --> 00:08:19,122
And if you forgot,

245
00:08:19,122 --> 00:08:20,349
you heard about it,

246
00:08:20,349 --> 00:08:21,512
gently but firmly.

247
00:08:21,862 --> 00:08:23,361
For the grandchildren,

248
00:08:23,361 --> 00:08:26,018
visits to grandma's house were magical.

249
00:08:26,198 --> 00:08:28,102
The cookie jar was always full.

250
00:08:28,282 --> 00:08:29,791
The rules were looser.

251
00:08:29,971 --> 00:08:31,971
Grandma let you stay up late,

252
00:08:31,971 --> 00:08:33,212
eat dessert first,

253
00:08:33,212 --> 00:08:34,453
and lick the bowl.

254
00:08:34,633 --> 00:08:36,814
You slept in a big feather bed

255
00:08:36,814 --> 00:08:38,487
under a handmade quilt.

256
00:08:38,667 --> 00:08:39,605
In the morning,

257
00:08:39,605 --> 00:08:41,293
you woke up to the smell of

258
00:08:41,293 --> 00:08:42,731
bacon and fresh coffee.

259
00:08:43,081 --> 00:08:45,948
The house was full of interesting things.

260
00:08:46,128 --> 00:08:48,401
An old trunk in the attic with

261
00:08:48,401 --> 00:08:50,749
army uniforms and love letters.

262
00:08:50,929 --> 00:08:53,054
A pump organ in the parlor.

263
00:08:53,234 --> 00:08:56,183
A wind-up Victrola with old records.

264
00:08:56,363 --> 00:09:00,171
Photographs of stern-looking ancestors on the walls.

265
00:09:00,351 --> 00:09:01,294
A button tin,

266
00:09:01,294 --> 00:09:03,616
full of every button ever saved,

267
00:09:03,616 --> 00:09:06,156
that children could sort for hours.

268
00:09:06,506 --> 00:09:10,139
Many grandparents also spoke another language.

269
00:09:10,319 --> 00:09:12,926
German, Italian, Polish,

270
00:09:12,926 --> 00:09:16,728
Swedish, Yiddish, Czech or Spanish.

271
00:09:16,908 --> 00:09:19,545
They had come to America as children,

272
00:09:19,545 --> 00:09:21,041
or their parents had.

273
00:09:21,221 --> 00:09:23,411
They spoke English with an accent,

274
00:09:23,411 --> 00:09:25,794
and switched to the old language when

275
00:09:25,794 --> 00:09:28,628
they didn't want the children to understand.

276
00:09:28,808 --> 00:09:31,737
Their grandchildren often learned a few words,

277
00:09:31,737 --> 00:09:34,346
mostly the ones they weren't supposed to.

278
00:09:34,697 --> 00:09:37,657
And grandparents connected children to history.

279
00:09:37,837 --> 00:09:40,362
A boy in nineteen sixty might have

280
00:09:40,362 --> 00:09:43,035
a grandfather who had seen Civil War

281
00:09:43,035 --> 00:09:44,966
veterans march in parades.

282
00:09:45,146 --> 00:09:47,154
A girl might have a grandmother who

283
00:09:47,154 --> 00:09:49,221
remembered when women couldn't vote.

284
00:09:49,401 --> 00:09:51,455
Women won the right to vote in

285
00:09:51,455 --> 00:09:54,879
nineteen twenty, and many grandmothers of nineteen

286
00:09:54,879 --> 00:09:57,481
sixty had cast their very first ballot

287
00:09:57,481 --> 00:09:58,166
that year.

288
00:09:58,517 --> 00:10:01,140
Holidays belonged to the grandparents.

289
00:10:01,320 --> 00:10:04,998
Thanksgiving and Christmas dinners at grandma's house

290
00:10:04,998 --> 00:10:06,871
were huge, with every aunt,

291
00:10:06,871 --> 00:10:09,647
uncle and cousin crammed into the dining

292
00:10:09,647 --> 00:10:11,589
room, and the kids at a card

293
00:10:11,589 --> 00:10:13,046
table in the kitchen.

294
00:10:13,226 --> 00:10:15,398
Grandma cooked for days beforehand.

295
00:10:15,578 --> 00:10:17,614
The turkey, the stuffing,

296
00:10:17,614 --> 00:10:20,140
the pies, and her famous rolls.

297
00:10:20,491 --> 00:10:23,085
After dinner, the men fell asleep in

298
00:10:23,085 --> 00:10:25,030
front of the football game.

299
00:10:25,210 --> 00:10:27,560
The women washed dishes and talked in

300
00:10:27,560 --> 00:10:28,322
the kitchen.

301
00:10:28,502 --> 00:10:30,746
The cousins ran wild outside,

302
00:10:30,746 --> 00:10:32,449
or explored the attic.

303
00:10:32,629 --> 00:10:35,193
And grandpa told the same stories he

304
00:10:35,193 --> 00:10:36,333
told every year,

305
00:10:36,333 --> 00:10:38,399
and everyone listened anyway.

306
00:10:38,749 --> 00:10:42,859
At Christmas, grandparents often gave practical gifts.

307
00:10:43,039 --> 00:10:45,291
Hand-knitted mittens and scarves.

308
00:10:45,471 --> 00:10:46,028
Socks.

309
00:10:46,208 --> 00:10:47,319
A savings bond,

310
00:10:47,319 --> 00:10:51,244
which the grandchildren didn't appreciate until years

311
00:10:51,244 --> 00:10:51,688
later.

312
00:10:51,868 --> 00:10:54,213
A crisp dollar bill in a card.

313
00:10:54,393 --> 00:10:56,331
And maybe one special toy,

314
00:10:56,331 --> 00:10:58,492
bought after weeks of saving.

315
00:10:58,672 --> 00:11:00,919
Those simple gifts are often the ones

316
00:11:00,919 --> 00:11:02,863
grandchildren remember the most.

317
00:11:03,213 --> 00:11:06,151
Growing old in nineteen sixty was harder

318
00:11:06,151 --> 00:11:07,915
than today in many ways.

319
00:11:08,095 --> 00:11:11,203
Life expectancy at birth was about seventy

320
00:11:11,203 --> 00:11:11,647
years.

321
00:11:11,827 --> 00:11:14,964
Heart disease, strokes and cancer took many

322
00:11:14,964 --> 00:11:16,715
people in their sixties.

323
00:11:16,895 --> 00:11:19,484
Hearing aids were big and clumsy.

324
00:11:19,664 --> 00:11:20,883
Eyeglasses were thick.

325
00:11:21,063 --> 00:11:23,465
And there were fewer treatments for the

326
00:11:23,465 --> 00:11:24,697
problems of old age.

327
00:11:25,047 --> 00:11:27,665
When grandparents grew too old or sick

328
00:11:27,665 --> 00:11:28,630
to live alone,

329
00:11:28,630 --> 00:11:31,525
they usually moved in with their children.

330
00:11:31,705 --> 00:11:33,104
Nursing homes existed,

331
00:11:33,104 --> 00:11:35,329
but they were much less common than

332
00:11:35,329 --> 00:11:35,710
today.

333
00:11:35,890 --> 00:11:37,922
Families took care of their own.

334
00:11:38,102 --> 00:11:41,731
Daughters, especially, cared for aging parents,

335
00:11:41,731 --> 00:11:42,967
often for years.

336
00:11:43,147 --> 00:11:44,714
It was simply expected.

337
00:11:45,064 --> 00:11:46,966
And when a grandparent died,

338
00:11:46,966 --> 00:11:49,207
the funeral was often held in the

339
00:11:49,207 --> 00:11:51,653
family home, or at the local funeral

340
00:11:51,653 --> 00:11:54,166
parlor, with a wake the night before.

341
00:11:54,346 --> 00:11:55,960
The whole town came.

342
00:11:56,140 --> 00:11:57,521
Neighbors brought food.

343
00:11:57,701 --> 00:11:59,917
Children saw death up close,

344
00:11:59,917 --> 00:12:01,974
as a natural part of life.

345
00:12:02,154 --> 00:12:03,740
And the old house was sold,

346
00:12:03,740 --> 00:12:06,031
and the family scattered a little more.

347
00:12:06,382 --> 00:12:08,944
So what did the grandparents of nineteen

348
00:12:08,944 --> 00:12:09,841
sixty give us?

349
00:12:10,021 --> 00:12:11,159
They gave us roots.

350
00:12:11,339 --> 00:12:13,512
A connection to a world that was

351
00:12:13,512 --> 00:12:14,938
already disappearing.

352
00:12:15,118 --> 00:12:16,712
They gave us their stories,

353
00:12:16,712 --> 00:12:19,367
their recipes, their quilts and their values.

354
00:12:19,547 --> 00:12:20,465
Hard work.

355
00:12:20,645 --> 00:12:21,527
Thrift.

356
00:12:21,707 --> 00:12:22,288
Faith.

357
00:12:22,468 --> 00:12:23,350
Family.

358
00:12:23,530 --> 00:12:24,528
Not complaining.

359
00:12:24,879 --> 00:12:26,947
And many of us didn't realize how

360
00:12:26,947 --> 00:12:29,267
much they meant until they were gone.

361
00:12:29,447 --> 00:12:31,595
We wish we had asked more questions.

362
00:12:31,775 --> 00:12:33,563
Written down the recipes.

363
00:12:33,743 --> 00:12:35,322
Recorded their voices.

364
00:12:35,502 --> 00:12:37,243
Learned the old language.

365
00:12:37,423 --> 00:12:39,809
If your grandparents are still in your

366
00:12:39,809 --> 00:12:42,133
memory, maybe it's time to write down

367
00:12:42,133 --> 00:12:43,263
what you remember,

368
00:12:43,263 --> 00:12:44,958
for your own grandchildren.

369
00:12:45,309 --> 00:12:47,085
So tell us in the comments.

370
00:12:47,265 --> 00:12:49,363
What do you remember most about your

371
00:12:49,363 --> 00:12:50,121
grandparents?

372
00:12:50,301 --> 00:12:52,060
What did your grandmother cook,

373
00:12:52,060 --> 00:12:54,330
and what did your grandfather teach you?

374
00:12:54,510 --> 00:12:55,777
We read every comment,

375
00:12:55,777 --> 00:12:57,679
and we'd love to hear about them.

376
00:12:57,859 --> 00:12:59,753
Next time, we're looking at what a

377
00:12:59,753 --> 00:13:02,259
dollar really bought in nineteen fifty-eight.

378
00:13:02,439 --> 00:13:04,158
Thank you for spending this time with

379
00:13:04,158 --> 00:13:04,297
me.
